  "response": "## The operation: `submitPayment` → `api.post(\"/charges\", …)`\n\n**File:** `src/services/payments.ts:11-23` (crimes `crime_00015`, `unsafe_retry` / \"Double Jeopardy\")\n\nNot `refundPayment` (`crime_00014`), even though the scan ranks it higher severity — a replayed `stripe.refunds.create` double-*refunds* a customer. The support report is double *charges*, and `/charges` is the charge path.\n\n## Evidence for why a replay is possible\n\nThe retry loop, verbatim:\n\n```ts\n11 │ export async function submitPayment(order) {\n12 │   for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 3; attempt++) {\n13 │     try {\n14 │       return await api.post(\"/charges\", {\n15 │         amount: order.total,\n16 │         timeout: timeoutSeconds,\n17 │       });\n18 │     } catch (e) {\n19 │       continue;\n20 │     }\n```\n\nThree facts make a replay a second charge:\n\n1. **The request carries nothing that identifies the operation.** The body is `{ amount, timeout }` — no order id, no key. From the provider's side, attempt 2 is indistinguishable from a genuine second purchase of the same amount. The scan states this as the primary missing safeguard: *\"no idempotency or deduplication key — nothing lets the receiver recognise a replayed attempt as the same operation.\"*\n2. **The catch cannot tell \"it failed\" from \"the response was lost.\"** `catch (e) { continue; }` retries every error, including a read timeout on a request the provider already committed. That is exactly the outage failure mode: the charge succeeded, the response never came back, the loop fired again. The scan: *\"also absent: error classification.\"* The file's own header comment already says it — *\"an attempt that succeeded on the server but timed out on the way back is charged twice.\"*\n3. **Nothing downstream catches the duplicate.** There is no transaction or atomic operation at the site (`\"transaction or atomic operation\"` listed as absent), and the only test (`payments.test.ts:10`) asserts `toHaveBeenCalled()` on a mock — it would pass just as happily if the charge fired three times (`crime_00018`, Mock Alibi).\n\nA \"handful of customers\" is the right shape for this bug: only the requests whose response was lost mid-flight get replayed, not every request during the outage.\n\n## The specific change\n\nSend a stable idempotency key, computed **once, outside the loop**, derived from the business operation:\n\n```ts\nexport async function submitPayment(order) {\n  // Same key on every attempt: the provider collapses replays of the\n  // same charge into one, even when our response was lost in flight.\n  const idempotencyKey = `charge:${order.id}`;\n\n  for (let attempt = 0; attempt < 3; attempt++) {\n    try {\n      return await api.post(\n        \"/charges\",\n        { amount: order.total, timeout: timeoutSeconds },\n        { headers: { \"Idempotency-Key\": idempotencyKey } },\n      );\n    } catch (e) {\n      continue;\n    }\n  }\n  console.log(chalk.red(\"payment failed\"));\n}\n```\n\nThree things the key must be, or it does nothing:\n\n- **Outside the loop.** Generating it inside — or using `crypto.randomUUID()` per attempt — produces a different key each time and the provider sees three distinct charges. This is the most common way this fix gets written wrong.\n- **Derived from the order, not the attempt.** `order.id` (or the checkout/payment-intent id). If the process crashes and the order is submitted again from a fresh call, the same key still suppresses the duplicate.\n- **Honoured by the receiver.** The key only works if `/charges` deduplicates on it. Confirm the header name the provider expects — Stripe-style APIs use `Idempotency-Key`, others take a `request_id` in the body.\n\nTwo things I could not verify in this fixture, so check them before merging: `src/lib/api.js` is imported but not present in the repo (`crime_00021` flags it as an undeclared import), so `api.post`'s third-argument signature is an assumption; and `submitPayment` is only ever called with `{ total }` in the test, so confirm real callers pass an order carrying a stable id. If they don't, the caller must supply one — a key invented inside `submitPayment` from the amount alone would wrongly collapse two legitimate identical purchases into one.\n\nWorth fixing in the same pass: `refundPayment` at line 26 has the identical defect (`withRetry` around `stripe.refunds.create`, no key), and Stripe's SDK takes `{ idempotencyKey }` as a second options argument.\n\n## Why backoff or fewer attempts would not fix it\n\nBoth change *when* and *how often* the replay happens. Neither changes what the provider does when it arrives.\n\n- **Backoff and jitter** space the attempts out. A duplicate charge sent 2 seconds later is still a duplicate charge — the provider has no way to recognise it, so it creates a second one. Backoff is a fix for *stampeding a struggling dependency*, which is a real and different problem (and worth adding alongside the key). It is not a fix for correctness.\n- **Lowering the attempt count** from 3 to 2 halves the number of extra charges in the worst case and removes exactly zero of them in the common one: the first attempt succeeds-then-times-out, the second replays it, the customer is charged twice. Going to 1 attempt \"fixes\" it only by deleting the retry — which reintroduces the failures the retry exists to absorb, and still leaves the first request's own ambiguity unresolved if the caller retries at a higher level.\n\nThe defect is not the retrying. It is that the receiver cannot tell a replay from a new request. Only something carried in the request that identifies the operation — the idempotency key — closes that gap; everything else just tunes the blast radius.",
